

**9. Bessel Functions of Integer Order**

**Mathematical Properties**

**Notation**

The tables in this chapter are for Bessel functions of integer order; the text treats general orders. The conventions used are:

\(z\)=\(x\)+ \(iy\); \(z\), \(y\) real.

\(n\) is a positive integer or zero.

\(r\), \(\mu\) are unrestricted except where otherwise indicated; \(r\) is supposed real in the sections devoted to Kelvin functions **9.9**, **9.10**, and **9.11**.

The notation used for the Bessel functions is that of Watson [9.15] and the British Association and Royal Society Mathematical Tables. The function \(Y_{*}(z)\) is often denoted \(N_{*}(z)\) by physicists and European workers.

Other notations are those of:

Aldis, Airey:

\(G_{n}(z)\) for \(-{\frac{1}{2}}\pi Y_{n}(z),K_{n}(z)\) for \((-)^{n}\!K_{n}(z)\).

Clifford:

\(C_{n}(z)\) for \(x^{-1}\!sJ_{n}(2\sqrt{z})\).

Gray, Mathews and MacRobert [9.9]:

\(\bar{Y}_{n}(z)\) for \({\frac{1}{2}}\pi Y_{n}(z)\)+ (ln \(2-\gamma J_{n}(z)\),

\(\bar{Y}_{*}(z)\) for \(\pi e^{\prime\prime i}\) sec\((\pi\pi)Y_{*}(z)\),

\(G_{n}(z)\) for \({\frac{1}{2}}\pi iH^{(1)}_{r}(z)\).

Jahnke, Emde and Losch [9.32]:

\(\Lambda_{*}(z)\) for \(\Gamma(v\!+\!1)({\frac{1}{2}}\varepsilon)^{-\gamma}J_{*}(z)\).

Jeffreys:

\(H_{s}(z)\) for \(H^{(1)}_{r}(z)\), \(H_{i*}(z)\) for \(H^{(2)}_{r}(z)\),

\(Kh_{s}(z)\) for \((2/\pi)K_{n}(z)\).

Heine:

\(K_{n}(z)\) for\(-{\frac{1}{2}}\pi Y_{n}(z)\).

Neumann:

\(Y^{n}(z)\) for \({\frac{1}{2}}\pi Y_{n}(z)\)+ (ln \(2-\gamma\))\(J_{n}(z)\).

Whittaker and Watson [9.18]:

\(K_{*}(z)\) for \(\cos(\nu\pi)K_{*}(z)\).

**Bessel Functions \(J\) and \(Y\)**

**9.1. Definitions and Elementary Properties**

**Differential Equation**

\(z^{2}\frac{dxw}{dz^{2}}\!+\!z\,\frac{dw}{dz}\!+\!(z^{2}\!\!-\!\!\nu^{2})w\!\!=\!0\)

Solutions are the Bessel functions of the first kind \(J_{\omega*}(z)\), of the second kind \(Y_{*}(z)\) (also called Weber's function) and of the third kind\(H^{(1)}_{r}(z)\), \(H^{(2)}_{r}(z)\) (also called the Hankel functions). Each is a regular (holomorphic) function of \(z\) throughout the \(z\)-plane cut along the negative real axis, and for fixed \(z(\neq\!0)\) each is an entire (integral) function of \(\nu\). When \(\nu\!=\!\pm n\), \(J_{*}(z)\) has no branch point and is an entire (integral) function of \(z\).

Important features of the various solutions are as follows: \(J_{*}(z)(\not{\partial}\nu\!\geq\!0)\) is bounded as \(z\!\to\!\!0\) in any bounded range of arg \(z\). \(J_{*}(z)\) and \(J_{*,*}(z)\) are linearly independent except when \(\nu\) is an integer. \(J_{*}(z)\) and \(Y_{*}(z)\) are linearly independent for all values of \(\nu\).

